+-# AnonSync slim C++ working cube — rev0666
++# AnonSync slim C++ working cube — rev0667
+ 
+ AnonSync is a **C++ peer-to-peer file synchronization system**. The product target is Resilio Sync-style local folder replication across authorized devices: discover peers, compare authenticated file manifests, transfer missing content-addressed chunks, apply creates/updates/deletes exactly once, preserve conflicts honestly, and resume after interruption without a central service being the ordinary source of truth.
+ 
+-The current C++ cube does not yet implement the whole sync product. It contains a security and durability substrate that a sync engine needs, plus the sync-domain model seam started in rev0651, tightened at folder-manifest level in rev0652, connected to live local folder scans in rev0653, extended in rev0654 with deterministic local/remote manifest diff planning, advanced in rev0655 with local apply planning, advanced in rev0656 with verified staged-file materialization, tightened in rev0657 with stale-target overwrite preflight, extended in rev0658 with safe remote tombstone application, extended in rev0659 with conflict-copy preservation for regular local files, extended in rev0660 with receipt-backed staged chunk writes, tightened in rev0661 with receipt-gated materialization, extended in rev0662 with staged-transfer inspection for resumable transfer, extended in rev0663 with deterministic chunk request planning for peer fetch batches, extended in rev0664 with requested peer-chunk acceptance, extended in rev0665 with response-envelope binding for individual peer chunk responses, and now extended in rev0666 with batch response-envelope acceptance so a peer can answer multiple selected chunks under one deterministic `sync-chunk-response-batch:v1:` commitment before bytes are staged. Existing authorization/replay/effect machinery remains subordinate to concrete sync mutations such as `publish_manifest`, `reserve_chunk_transfer`, `commit_file_version`, `apply_tombstone`, and `record_conflict`.
++The current C++ cube does not yet implement the whole sync product. It contains a security and durability substrate that a sync engine needs, plus the sync-domain model seam started in rev0651, tightened at folder-manifest level in rev0652, connected to live local folder scans in rev0653, extended in rev0654 with deterministic local/remote manifest diff planning, advanced in rev0655 with local apply planning, advanced in rev0656 with verified staged-file materialization, tightened in rev0657 with stale-target overwrite preflight, extended in rev0658 with safe remote tombstone application, extended in rev0659 with conflict-copy preservation for regular local files, extended in rev0660 with receipt-backed staged chunk writes, tightened in rev0661 with receipt-gated materialization, extended in rev0662 with staged-transfer inspection for resumable transfer, extended in rev0663 with deterministic chunk request planning for peer fetch batches, extended in rev0664 with requested peer-chunk acceptance, extended in rev0665 with response-envelope binding for individual peer chunk responses, extended in rev0666 with batch response-envelope acceptance, and now extended in rev0667 with a post-batch transfer-round continuation seam that accepts a valid multi-chunk peer reply, re-inspects staged receipt state, and deterministically builds the next request plan or marks the staged file ready to materialize. Existing authorization/replay/effect machinery remains subordinate to concrete sync mutations such as `publish_manifest`, `reserve_chunk_transfer`, `commit_file_version`, `apply_tombstone`, and `record_conflict`.
+ 
+-Rev0666 keeps the code in C++ and fixes the next transfer/session gap: rev0665 modeled a single peer response envelope, but a real sync session should be able to accept a bounded set of chunk responses from one request batch without treating each response as an unrelated call. Rev0666 adds `SyncChunkResponseBatchEnvelope`, `SyncChunkResponseBatchAcceptanceResult`, `build_sync_chunk_response_batch_envelope`, and `accept_sync_chunk_response_batch_envelope`. The new boundary rejects duplicate/out-of-order response chunks, forged batch IDs, mismatched response counts/byte totals, wrong byte hashes, and stale request evidence before staging peer bytes through the existing receipt-backed writer.
++Rev0667 keeps the code in C++ and fixes the next transfer/session gap: rev0666 could safely accept a batch, but the caller still had to separately rediscover what changed after the write. Rev0667 adds `SyncChunkTransferRoundResult` and `accept_sync_chunk_response_batch_and_plan_next`, which require matching write/inspection roots, route the batch through `accept_sync_chunk_response_batch_envelope`, re-run `inspect_sync_staged_transfer`, rebuild the next `sync-chunk-request:v1:` plan from verified post-batch state, and report whether more chunks are needed or the staged file is materialization-ready.
