-## Known ceiling after rev0666
+## Known ceiling after rev0667
 
-Rev0666 adds batch response-envelope-bound chunk acceptance. It still does not persist transfer state in SQLite, exchange manifests with peers, run a peer session loop, clean up orphan `.part` or `.chunks` receipt directories, execute local-tombstone conflict semantics, watch the OS filesystem, or run as a complete sync daemon. Python remains packaging/validation glue; product code belongs in `cpp/anonsync_core`.
+Rev0667 adds post-batch transfer-round continuation on top of batch response-envelope-bound chunk acceptance. It still does not persist transfer state in SQLite, exchange manifests with peers, run a peer session loop, clean up orphan `.part` or `.chunks` receipt directories, execute local-tombstone conflict semantics, watch the OS filesystem, or run as a complete sync daemon. Python remains packaging/validation glue; product code belongs in `cpp/anonsync_core`.
 
 ## Rev0666 chunk response batch
 
 Rev0666 adds `SyncChunkResponseBatchEnvelope`, `SyncChunkResponseBatchAcceptanceResult`, `build_sync_chunk_response_batch_envelope`, and `accept_sync_chunk_response_batch_envelope`. The batch envelope carries the deterministic `sync-chunk-response-batch:v1:` ID, the `sync-chunk-request:v1:` ID being answered, remote entry/version digests, apply intent ID, response count, total bytes, and the ordered individual `SyncChunkResponseEnvelope` values. Acceptance rebuilds the request plan from inspection/options evidence, rebuilds the expected batch envelope, rejects forged batch IDs or duplicate/out-of-order response chunks, prevalidates every response byte length/hash, and only then stages bytes through the individual response-envelope and receipt-backed write path.
+
+Rev0667 adds `SyncChunkTransferRoundResult` and `accept_sync_chunk_response_batch_and_plan_next` so the fake peer/session layer can accept a batch, re-inspect verified staged receipt state, and deterministically return either the next `sync-chunk-request:v1:` plan or a materialization-ready signal without duplicating continuation logic outside the C++ sync boundary.
